1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,438
The missing voice belonged to a woman with a serious face who had been paid off in the last layoff.

2
00:00:05,438 --> 00:00:12,112
She came to the office with a purse, a folder, and a paragraph she had written by hand.

3
00:00:12,112 --> 00:00:17,196
“You are printing a story about people like me,” she said.

4
00:00:17,196 --> 00:00:22,445
“I would like to see the text before it goes to the printer.”

5
00:00:22,445 --> 00:00:25,645
The team assumed she wanted money.

6
00:00:25,645 --> 00:00:28,289
She wanted accuracy.

7
00:00:28,289 --> 00:00:33,264
“The collection of interviews you have is a drama,” she said.

8
00:00:33,264 --> 00:00:35,459
“It is not a poll.

9
00:00:35,459 --> 00:00:44,192
My name is on your list, and I am available to be interviewed properly, together with the others.”

10
00:00:44,192 --> 00:00:53,251
She was right about the diagram, too: the arrows pointed upwards, and the payroll line went downwards.

11
00:00:53,251 --> 00:01:11,323
“Regulate that,” she said, “or your illustration will do the work of a layoff.” A mature engineer from the founding team agreed, and the young kid from the mailroom, who had expected a special bonus, went quiet.

12
00:01:11,323 --> 00:01:19,132
She asked one question that the panel could not answer: who buys a house on a zero-hours contract?

13
00:01:19,132 --> 00:01:24,061
“The means are not the same as the opportunity,” she said.

14
00:01:24,061 --> 00:01:41,084
“Your film will deliver a story about insurance and copyright, and it will miss the sale of a person's whole week.” The office was open and idle that afternoon; the craze for the platform had not yet reached the third floor.

15
00:01:41,084 --> 00:01:45,538
They gave her the text and the location of every print run.

16
00:01:45,538 --> 00:01:49,517
“I am glad you asked,” Maya said.

17
00:01:49,517 --> 00:01:53,512
“I am not asking,” the woman replied.

18
00:01:53,512 --> 00:02:03,485
“I am telling you what an appropriate interview looks like.” The interactive apparatus in the corner hummed; nobody looked at it.

19
00:02:03,485 --> 00:02:12,489
The paragraph she had written was better than the one they had, and they used it, including her name at the top.

20
00:02:12,489 --> 00:02:22,283
For the panel, the interview was also a test of the illustration: was the diagram accurate, and did it serve the reader?

21
00:02:22,283 --> 00:02:32,551
A mature engineer from the founding team said the print run was too early, and that the payroll should be regulated before the sale of any subscription.

22
00:02:32,551 --> 00:02:36,636
“You assume a small office,” she said.

23
00:02:36,636 --> 00:02:49,413
“Allow me to say that a kid from the mailroom can see what a copyright does to a hire.” They were glad to be corrected, together, in a spot the apparatus could record.

24
00:02:49,413 --> 00:02:56,387
The purse stayed closed; the drama, at least, was now available to buy.

25
00:02:56,387 --> 00:03:14,663
The teacher of the evening class, who had left the industry two years later, said the business of the field was to protect the reader, and that no royalty, however effective, could engage a person who had already turned away.
